{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "03", "section": "02", "chapter_title": "Data Storytelling With Excel", "title": "Formulas for Everyday Analysis", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting formulas for everyday analysis to Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel", "cat100_supplement_formulas_for_everyday_analysis", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 2, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/03/01"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_3_2_formulas_for_everyday_analysis_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_3_2_formulas_for_everyday_analysis_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_3_2_formulas_for_everyday_analysis_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_3_2_formulas_for_everyday_analysis_calibration"]}, "content": "# Formulas for Everyday Analysis\n\n![Formulas for Everyday Analysis textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/03-02-formulas-for-everyday-analysis.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA formula is the difference between a spreadsheet that stores numbers and one that computes with them. The moment you write `=B3*C3` instead of typing the answer, the cell becomes live: change an input and the result updates, copy the formula down and it adapts to each row. That single shift, from typed values to referenced values, is the foundation of every analysis later in this module.\n\nThe skill that separates a working sheet from a broken one is understanding how references move when you copy a formula. Excel offers two behaviors, relative and absolute, and choosing the wrong one is the most common cause of a sheet that looks right at the top and goes silently wrong further down. This section teaches you to read a reference, predict how it will shift, and lock the parts that must stay fixed.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nYou build an order sheet. In D3 you write `=B3*C3` for the first line total, it returns the right number, and you copy it down the column. Every line total looks correct. Then you add a shipping fee with `=D3+F1`, copy that down too, and the first row is fine but every row below it is wrong: the shipping silently drops to zero. You did not change the data. The formula moved in a way you did not intend.\n\nThis is the quiet trap of relative references. When you copy `=D3+F1` from row 3 to row 4, Excel rewrites it to `=D4+F2`, and F2 is empty. The fix is to know when to write `$F$1` so the reference stays put. Learning to see that shift before it bites is what this section is for.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA cell reference like `B3` is an address, and a formula is a recipe written in terms of addresses rather than values. The key mechanic is what happens to those addresses when you copy the formula to another cell. Excel adjusts them by the same offset you moved.\n\nThere are two reference styles, and the dollar sign is the switch between them:\n\n| Reference | Written as | When copied down one row | Use it for |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Relative | `B3` | Becomes `B4` | A per-row input that should track each row |\n| Absolute | `$B$3` | Stays `B3` | A single shared value (a tax rate, a fixed fee) |\n| Mixed | `B$3` or `$B3` | Locks only the row or only the column | Filling a grid across both directions |\n\nRead the dollar sign as a lock. `$F$1` means \"lock the column F and the row 1, never move.\" A reference with no dollar signs is fully free and shifts in whatever direction you copy.\n\nBeyond references, three functions cover most everyday analysis: `SUM(range)` adds a range, `AVERAGE(range)` means it, and `COUNT(range)` tallies the numeric cells. A useful warning: `AVERAGE` ignores blank cells but a stray zero is counted, so a blank and a zero give different averages. Knowing that distinction keeps your means honest.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nSuppose an order sheet looks like this. Quantity is in column B, unit price in column C, and the shipping fee for all rows is fixed in cell F1 = 5.\n\n```\n   A         B    C       D            E\n1  Shipping  5\n2  Item      Qty  Price   Line Total   With Ship\n3  Mug       3    4.00     ?            ?\n4  Pen       10   1.50     ?            ?\n```\n\nStep 1. In D3 write `=B3*C3`. That returns `3*4.00 = 12.00`. Copy D3 down to D4. Because B3 and C3 are relative, D4 becomes `=B4*C4 = 10*1.50 = 15.00`.\n\nStep 2. In E3 write `=D3+$F$1`. The `$F$1` is absolute, so copying E3 down keeps pointing at the one shipping cell. E3 = `12.00 + 5 = 17.00` and E4 = `15.00 + 5 = 20.00`. If you had written `=D3+F1` instead, E4 would read `=D4+F2`, F2 is empty, and the shipping silently drops to 0.\n\nStep 3. Total the orders with `=SUM(E3:E4)`, which returns `37.00`.\n\nThe decision a reader can audit: relative references (`B3`, `C3`) for per-row inputs, an absolute reference (`$F$1`) for the one shared fee. Mixing those up is the single most common formula error in this section.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Build and copy the order sheet without the shipping bug\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"In D3 you need the line total for Mug (Qty 3, Price 4.00). Which formula, and why these reference styles?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"=B3*C3 (both relative, each row has its own qty and price)\",\"=$B$3*$C$3 (lock both so the total never changes)\",\"=B3*C3*F1 (multiply in shipping here)\",\"Type 12.00 directly\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"=B3*C3 with both relative. Each row owns its quantity and price, so when you copy down, D4 correctly becomes =B4*C4 = 15.00.\"},{\"prompt\":\"In E3 you add the fixed shipping in F1=5. Which formula survives copying down?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"=D3+F1\",\"=D3+$F$1\",\"=$D$3+F1\",\"=D3+F$1 only works going sideways\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"=D3+$F$1. The absolute $F$1 stays pinned, so E4 becomes =D4+$F$1 = 20.00. Writing =D3+F1 would slide to =D4+F2 (empty), silently dropping shipping to zero.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Total with =SUM(E3:E4) = 37.00. The auditable pattern: relative for per-row inputs, absolute for the one shared fee.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nThe reference decision is only safe if you also test the fill. Order the copy-and-verify procedure a reviewer would follow.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Decide per reference: should it move with the fill or stay fixed?\",\"Lock shared constants with \\\\$ (the one fee or rate)\",\"Copy the formula down the column\",\"Click the LAST filled cell and read the formula bar\",\"Confirm locked references still point where intended\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore you copy a formula, ask one question of every reference in it: should this address move with the fill, or stay fixed? Each input that belongs to the row stays relative; each value that is shared by all rows gets locked with dollar signs. Then test the bottom of the column, not just the top, because relative-reference errors hide below the first row.\n\nUse this check on any column of copied formulas:\n\n1. **Per-row inputs:** Are quantities, prices, and other row-specific values written as relative references so they track each row?\n2. **Shared constants:** Is every value used by all rows (a fee, a rate, a target) locked with `$`, so the fill keeps pointing at the one cell?\n3. **Bottom-row test:** Click the last filled cell and read its formula. Does it still reference the cells you intended, or did a relative reference drift onto a blank cell?\n\nIf the last row's formula points somewhere unexpected, you have a reference-locking error, not a data error.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nA frequent misreading is believing a formula is correct because the first result is correct. The first row often works by coincidence: `=D3+F1` is right in row 3 because F1 happens to hold the fee. The error only appears in row 4 when the reference slides to the empty F2. Always verify a copied formula at the bottom of its range.\n\nA second misreading is confusing a typed answer with a computed one. If you type `12.00` into a line-total cell instead of `=B3*C3`, the number looks identical but will not update when the quantity changes. Reading the formula bar, not just the cell, tells you whether a value is live or frozen.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the relative-versus-absolute decision to a study-budget sheet. Column B lists item costs, and cell E1 holds the sales-tax rate of 0.08. You want a \"cost with tax\" column.\n\nIn C2 you write `=B2*(1+$E$1)`. The `B2` is relative because each row has its own cost, and `$E$1` is absolute because every row uses the one tax rate. Copy C2 down: C3 becomes `=B3*(1+$E$1)`, exactly what you want, the cost reference advancing while the rate stays pinned. Had you written `=B2*(1+E1)`, the copy would become `=B3*(1+E2)`, and E2 is blank, so row 3's \"tax\" would vanish.\n\nVerify at the bottom. Click the last filled cell, read the formula bar, and confirm the rate still says `$E$1`. Then total the column with `=SUM(C2:C20)` so a reader can re-add the line items and reach the same grand total.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader can look at a formula and narrate what it will become two rows down before pressing copy. Practice predicting the fill:\n\n1. `=A2+B2` copied to row 3 becomes `=A3+B3` (both shift).\n2. `=A2*$B$1` copied to row 3 becomes `=A3*$B$1` (only the free reference shifts).\n3. `=A2/A$1` copied to row 3 becomes `=A3/A$1` (the locked row stays, the column would still shift if copied sideways).\n\nWhen you can read a formula and forecast its copied form, you can debug a wrong column in seconds instead of retyping it.\n\n<inline-check question=\"You copy =B2*(1+$E$1) from row 2 down to row 3. What does row 3 become?\" explanation=\"The relative B2 advances to B3, but the absolute $E$1 stays pinned to the one tax-rate cell. So row 3 reads =B3*(1+$E$1), exactly what you want. Had you written E1 unlocked, it would slide to the blank E2 and the tax would vanish.\" options='[{\"text\":\"=B3*(1+$E$1) (the rate stays locked, the cost advances)\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"=B3*(1+$E$2) (both references shift down)\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"=B2*(1+$E$1) (nothing changes when copied)\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_formulas_for_everyday_analysis\", \"cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong formula artifact does not just show the final totals; it makes the reference logic legible. Label the shared constant clearly (a cell named \"Tax rate\" or \"Shipping fee\"), and in your trace note which cells are locked and why. A model line reads: \"Shipping is fixed for all orders, so I locked it as `$F$1`; the per-row inputs B and C stay relative so each line total reflects its own quantity and price.\"\n\nThe weak version pastes a screenshot of right-looking numbers. The strong version lets a reviewer click any computed cell, read the formula, and confirm the lock pattern matches the logic you described. The proof lives in the formula bar, not the displayed value.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nThe same locking logic transfers to a multiplication grid, where you lock differently. Suppose row 1 holds prices across columns and column A holds quantities down the rows, and you want each interior cell to be quantity times price. The formula in the first interior cell is `=$A2*B$1`: the column of A is locked (`$A`) so the quantity always comes from column A, and the row of 1 is locked (`B$1`) so the price always comes from row 1. This mixed reference, locking one direction each, is the natural extension of the single-cell absolute reference, and recognizing when you need it shows you understand references rather than memorizing one pattern.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/03-02-formulas-for-everyday-analysis-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"03.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **NACE career readiness**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title for CAT 100 03.02. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to ISTE digital citizenship. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_formulas_for_everyday_analysis\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links claim, evidence, tool boundary, revision, and remaining limitation. That is what separates digital fluency from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_3_2_formulas_for_everyday_analysis_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_formulas_for_everyday_analysis", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_formulas_for_everyday_analysis", "cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel"], "stem": "Cell D2 holds the formula =B2*C2 